> On Wed, Apr 7, 2010 at 01:03, And Rosta and.rosta@gmail.com> wrote:


> [* As I see it, the design problem has two parts. Both have to do with  
> finding ways to logically precise meanings concise enough to be worth the  
> effort of verbalizing. One part is to find a more concise way of of  
> encoding variables than standard predicate logic notation and Lojban  
> offer, given that in most propositions we express (in natural language)  
> there are many variables and each variable tends to be argument of many  
> predicates. The other part is to devise an inventory of predicates that  
> expand to more complex logical structures.]



> I don't really see your case here; if one of the basic goals is to be a  
> useful human language, then I don't see any alternative to predicate  
> logic as two-dimensional representation of utterances. Or, if there was  
> one. it would seem inherently illogical due to it's complexity. Could you  
> (or anyone) expand this thought?








